00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.369 align:start position:0%
this is Enceladus Saturn's sixth largest  moon at around 500 kilometers in  diameter Enceladus is encased in a crust  of ice up to 40 kilometers thick loading  atop of vast salty ocean from afar  Enceladus looks like a smooth white  pearl but a closer look at its surface  reveals a rougher reality its North Pole  is riddled with impact craters scars  left over from run-ins with asteroids  and other cosmic debris run until this  is South Pole we see long aquamarine  tiger stripes fractures in the ice since  few tall plumes of water vapor and ice  particles kilometers above the surface  since 2005 Cassini has been gathering  and analyzed material from these plumes  we know they contain water vapour and  traces of nitrogen and methane but the  current mission was to look for  molecular hydrogen here on earth  organisms known as a chemo Litha troves  have the ability to harvest chemical  energy from hydrogen forming a base of  life independent of sunlight in this  latest plume dive Cassini screens past  Enceladus a South Pole at around eight  and a half kilometres a second a mere 50  kilometres above the surface at its  lowest point gas captured during the  flyby revealed that these plumes do  contain hydrogen gas this suggests that  Enceladus is rocky core is interacting  with the warm waters of the subsurface  ocean such an interaction makes  Enceladus a more suitable environment  for life than any other body in the  solar system outside of Earth this was  unfortunately one of Cassini's last  missions NASA will soon send the  satellite on a series of 22 dives  between the planet and its rings a  region of Saturn yet to be explored  these dangerous dyes will reveal new  information about the formation of  Saturn's rings are they as old as the  solar system or a new addition during  its last five orbits Cassini will dip a  toe into Saturn's atmosphere  in September Ksenia set the plunge of  the Saturn sending back what  measurements it can before it vaporizes  bringing to a close a 20-year  exploration of one of our solar system's  largest residents  [Music]
 

